	Let us call the next case here the "post-transfer" case. This case is significantly less artificial than the one before; its starting conditions reflect those of late stages of language endangerment.
	Consider again, a scenario involving different generations, wherein there occurs a break in the continuity of a first language. As opposed to the previous case, let us suppose this time that when referring to the elder generation, the elders in question are members of the community starting from about age 65 or so. Further, these elders are bilingual. They are the last remaining speakers of their first language L1x, and they also also speak the younger generations' first language, L1y. Meanwhile, the youth and the adults in this case are monolingual, only speaking L1y. We shall take the youth and the adults together to be all those younger than 65 who are also old enough to have already aquired L1y as a first language.
	Now imagine that for one reason or another the youth and the adults alike undertake to learn L1x from their elders, and with enough time a good amount of them do manage to become bilingual in L1y and L1x, though we should assume that none or very few of them reach the kind of first-language fluency that matches what they possess with L1y. Then, some time after this transfer of the language has occurred, the elders eventually pass away, leaving behind the younger generations, wherein L1x is only spoken as a second language.
	What we are considering here, then, can be seen as a case of language learning as an ameliorative project, which serves to provide the elders with as much or as close to the first-language L1x community that they otherwise would have lost access to entirely. As discussed in the previous scenario, I think that Nowak's autonomy-centric considerations could work to motivate a project such as the one we are now considering. However, the same considerations that get the project off the ground while the elders are still living, do not here apply to the adults and the youth.
	Since the youth and adults are second-language speakers, the same counterveiling factor that I discussed in the previous case applies again here. The youth and adults, at worst, have an interest against continuing to speak L1x after the elders pass away because L1x is not their first language. At the very least, the argument for preserving first languages cannot seem to provide per se a reason for the youth and adults to continue speaking L1x. Without looking to other considerations, it seems that the youth and adults might just as well forgo L1x, or at least, it would be no loss in the sense which Nowak's argument registers. And in this case, whatever additional considerations might have been in play about intergenerational intelligibility with the first-language speakers no longer apply once the first-language speakers have passed away.
	